;;; Lazy.mon --- Pure call-by-need evaluation strategy

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.2.0
:keywords lazy call-by-need thunk sharing suspension memoization evaluation

;;; Commentary:

 Lazy is Monad's pure call-by-need evaluation strategy. It is a general Core
 abstraction, not a sequence feature. A Lazy value may be used at any recursive
 position: sequence tails, tree branches, graph neighborhoods, parser states,
 memoized proof objects, or user-defined structures that do not yet exist.

 The compiler owns only the suspension substrate. Core owns the algebra built
 on that substrate. =delay expression= captures EXPRESSION without evaluating
 it. =force value= demands the captured computation. The first successful force
 evaluates the body and memoizes the result; later forces observe the shared
 result rather than re-running the body.

;;; Call-by-Need Semantics

 Lazy differs from call-by-name precisely by sharing. Operationally, a thunk
 has states analogous to

                    Unevaluated closure
                    Evaluating
                    Evaluated value

 Force atomically moves an unrestricted thunk from Unevaluated through
 Evaluating to Evaluated. Re-entering a thunk while it is Evaluating is a
 black hole and therefore denotes divergence rather than a fabricated value.
 Concurrent forcing of an unrestricted thunk must not evaluate its body more
 than once.

 The observable pure-value law is

                    force (delay e)  ≃  e

 but this equation must not be used to justify transformations that duplicate
 E, evaluate E earlier than demanded, or destroy sharing.

;;; Relation to Guarded Recursion

 Lazy is operational suspension. It is intentionally *not* identified with
 Nakano's proof-theoretic later modality ▷ because ordinary =force= eliminates
 Lazy whenever the program demands it. A future guarded-recursion checker may
 interpret selected Lazy occurrences as guarded recursive positions and prove
 productivity, but those proofs live above this operational substrate.

 This separation matters for research-grade soundness: useful operational
 laziness and a logical productivity modality are related, but they are not the
 same type former unless the language also controls elimination by clocks or an
 equivalent discipline.

;;; Effects and Quantitative Ownership

 The body of delay may be total or may carry only pure divergence. It may not
 perform externally observable effects such as IO, mutation, exceptions, or
 foreign execution: memoization would otherwise change effect multiplicity.
 Force therefore carries a latent =diverge= effect unless the compiler proves
 the particular thunk total. Effect-polymorphic suspensions, if Monad later
 needs them, should be a separate type with explicit sharing semantics.

 Lazy preserves quantitative ownership. A thunk that closes over a quantity-1
 value is itself usable only at a quantity compatible with that capture. The
 compiler may memoize unrestricted thunks, but it may not turn memoization into
 implicit duplication of linear resources.

;;; Compiler Contract

 The compiler provides exactly:

   Lazy  :: Type -> Type
   delay :: <special-form> a -> Lazy a
   force :: Lazy a -> a

 Required properties:

   1. delay does not evaluate its body when the suspension is constructed.
   2. force evaluates an unrestricted suspension at most once.
   3. force (delay e) returns the same pure value as e when e terminates.
   4. recursive self-force is detected as black-hole divergence.
   5. shared forcing is thread-safe for unrestricted thunks.
   6. QTT usage of captured values is preserved.
   7. the representation is unobservable and may be erased by specialization.
   8. no data structure receives a privileged lazy runtime representation.

 Everything else below is ordinary Core semantics.

;;; Research Basis

 John Launchbury, "A Natural Semantics for Lazy Evaluation", POPL 1993.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/158511.158618

 Zena Ariola and Matthias Felleisen, "The Call-by-Need Lambda Calculus",
 Journal of Functional Programming, 1997.
   https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796898003037

 Robert Atkey and Conor McBride, "Productive Coprogramming with Guarded
 Recursion", ICFP 2013.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/2500365.2500597

 The first two references motivate sharing-sensitive call-by-need semantics.
 The third motivates a future type-based productivity layer without collapsing
 operational Lazy into a logical later modality.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Control.Applicative

module Control.Lazy where
  :compiler-substrate
    Lazy delay force


instance Functor Lazy
  map f value -> delay (f (force value))


instance Applicative Lazy
  pure value -> delay value
  applyA f value -> delay ((force f) (force value))


instance Evaluation Lazy
  defer thunk -> delay (thunk ())
  demand value -> force value


tests
  :call-by-need-surface:
  assert-eq (force (delay (1 + 2))) 3 "Lazy delay/force preserves the pure value"
  assert-eq (force (map (lambda (x) (x * 2)) (delay 4))) 8 "Lazy Functor maps under suspension"
  assert-eq (force (liftA2 (lambda (x y) (x + y)) (delay 3) (delay 5))) 8 "Lazy Applicative combines suspended values"
  assert-eq (demand-total (total-defer (lambda (_) 11))) 11 "TotalDemand discharges a total Lazy demand"
  :call-by-need-surface:
